# EVERY (pack_id, version) THAT HAS EVER BEEN PUBLISHED — one `pack_id@version` per line (#708).
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. A session PINS `(pack_id, version)` for the length of an interview, and
# `reply-closure.json` — the TTS manifest both apps/api and apps/ai-service read — is built from
# whatever the corpus holds at build time. So a version that stops existing does not merely go
# unused: every worker mid-interview on it is served strings that were never checked and never
# rendered. On the voice form that is silence, at a question they cannot read.
#
# Nothing derived from the current corpus can notice a disappearance, which is why this is a
# ledger rather than a computed set. `reply-closure.golden.test.ts` fails the build when a line
# here no longer resolves to a pack on disk.
#
# ADDING a version: create the pack file, then regenerate —
#   UPDATE_PACK_VERSIONS=1 pnpm --filter @badabhai/api run test reply-closure.golden
# RETIRING one: delete its line by hand. That diff is a reviewable claim that no live session
# pins it any more — which is exactly the sort of thing that should never be asserted silently.
#
# NAMING, for anything that is not v1: `packs/<pack_id>@<version>.json`. The loader reads every
# `packs/*.json` and does not care about the filename, so the bare `<pack_id>.json` form the 101
# v1 packs use keeps working; the suffix is what stops a second version from being published by
# overwriting the first, which is the mistake this whole file exists to make loud.
